2b:["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-3 rounded-lg border border-sky-500/25 bg-sky-500/[0.06] px-4 py-2.5 text-sm text-zinc-200 leading-relaxed","children":"The front-load-then-taper question, answered by the same engine: taper to 12h after day 13 and the month keeps only 45.6% of the flat-22h gain; taper to 18h after day 13 and 84.1% of that gain survives on 80% of flat-22h's extra hours. Flat 24h remains max output; plain Sundays-on (12h × 30d) remains the best hours-to-litres conversion at 82.7%."}]
2c:["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-3 space-y-1 text-[11px] text-zinc-500","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-amber-300/80","children":"Sunday wages are not modelled in any of these patterns — “cheapest” is about hours converting to litres, not wage cost."}],["$","div",null,{"children":["pct_extra_hours_used = (line-hours used beyond baseline) / (extra line-hours offered); offered = scenario available line-hours − baseline available line-hours, where available = shift ceiling × working days × number of lines, and a tapered pattern uses its per-phase ceilings (front through the switch day, tail after) derived from its own day files; every figure computed from the scenario artifacts","."]}],["$","div",null,{"children":["every pattern shown has a full 30-day artifact on disk (summary, day files, events) — including the 18h-tail taper an earlier build flagged as missing","."]}]]}]
